      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Multiplying through by $x,$ we get $\\tan x (x) + \\sin x (x) = \\frac{4}{3} (x).$  Then\n\\[\\frac{\\sin ^2 x}{\\cos ^2 x} + \\sin x = \\frac{4}{3} (x).\\]Multiplying through by $\\cos ^2 x,$ we get\n\\[\\sin ^2 x + \\sin x \\cos ^2 x = \\frac{4}{3} (x) \\cos ^2 x.\\]Using the identity $\\sin ^2 x + \\cos ^2 x = 1,$ we have $\\sin x (\\cos ^2 x - 1) = \\frac{4}{3} (x) \\cos ^2 x - \\sin x \\cos ^2 x.$  Since $\\cos ^2 x > 0$ for all $x,$ this is equivalent to $\\sin x = \\frac{\\sin x \\cos ^2 x}{4/3 - 1} = \\frac{3 \\cos x}{-1/3} = -9 \\cos x.$  Then\n\\[\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = -9 \\sin x,\\]so\n\\begin{align*}\n-9 \\sin ^2 x &= \\cos ^2 x \\\\\n9 \\sin ^2 x &= - \\cos ^2 x \\\\\n9 (\\sin ^2 x + \\cos ^2 x) &= - \\cos ^2 x \\\\\n9 &= - \\cos ^2 x \\\\\n\\cos x &= \\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n\\end{align*}Thus, \\[\\sin x = \\pm \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\]The answer is \\boxed{\\frac{9 \\sqrt{10}}{10}, -\\frac{9 \\sqrt{10}}{10}}.",
